WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.067
The cut is the total weight of the wires between clusters.

2
00:00:08.067 --> 00:00:13.333
Merge P and Q, and only the wires between them become inside wires.

3
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:19.244
Nothing else changes, so the cut drops by exactly the affinity of P and Q.

4
00:00:19.244 --> 00:00:28.267
Taking the highest affinity makes the cut fall the most at each step: steepest descent, with no global guarantee.

5
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:32.800
Using a sum as the affinity has a side effect.

6
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:40.933
ABC and D share three thin wires, 3 in all, beating E–F's 2; as an average it is only 1.

7
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:44.178
On the 24-cell circuit:

8
00:00:44.178 --> 00:00:49.444
the first 7 steps all feed the same cluster. Big clusters snowball.

9
00:00:49.844 --> 00:00:56.644
Comparing selection keys: a tuple compares field by field, and the first field settles it.

10
00:00:56.644 --> 00:01:05.000
A string only sees characters: the second character “1” decides, and 10 versus 9 is never asked.

11
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:14.467
JavaScript's default sort() does the same: 10, 9, 2 becomes 10, 2, 9.

12
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:19.667
No capacity, K = 2: legal, and K is reached.

13
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:25.356
Capacity 2: legal, but the positive pairs left are all over capacity.

14
00:01:25.356 --> 00:01:30.689
A disconnected graph: no wire at all between the remaining clusters.

15
00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:35.822
Report all three separately, and stop when no legal pair remains.

16
00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:43.244
Greedy never backtracks. Module 04 has an example: capacity 2, K = 2.

17
00:01:43.244 --> 00:01:50.933
A–B goes first, the largest at 10; C and D are left together, cut 18.

18
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:55.067
Yet AC | BD has a cut of only 11.

19
00:01:55.067 --> 00:02:01.422
The best first step left only bad choices. The next module is about exactly this.

20
00:02:01.822 --> 00:02:06.622
Cost: merge a 12×12 grid down to 2 clusters.

21
00:02:06.622 --> 00:02:14.889
Recomputing every pair each step costs about 7 times as much as updating only the pairs next to the new cluster.

22
00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:21.689
Because no other pair's affinity changed: use a priority queue and update only those few.

23
00:02:22.089 --> 00:02:24.156
Six things to keep.

24
00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:40.400
Next: capacity takes centre stage.
